it's a long and difficult route up to  the mountains of Panay the mountains  ascend more than 1000 meters there are  no roads the only way up is on foot only  a few corners of forest remain the rest  has been logged or burned down replaced  by rice paddies other crops and grassy  hillsides in general this forest is  their habitat the home of the wildlife  species and biodiversity so once it is  disturbed or gone their home will be  gone as well so it's very important  there are still many native species in  the forest the two environmentalists are  especially interested in the rafflesia  it's the world's largest flower and is  very rare it gives off a scent like  rotting flesh that draws the insects  which pollinate the flowers endemic here  once button sit they'll be done here in  the island you can't find them in other  places in other islands in the  Philippines and in the whole world so  it's very important to protect this area  especially that there are a lot of  threats around this area threats to the  biodiversity not only in this specific  species but to the whole biodiversity  estou the village of kario is about two  hours away on foot the people are  farmers and subsist from agriculture  most live in wood huts it's a simple way  of life and at first glance it seems  unchanging but traces of last year's  typhoon haiyan are still visible  everywhere once this was a small complex  for visitors  the typhoon destroyed it all homes a  cafeteria a small pond all swept away  and the environmentalists find more  signs of improper reforesting instead of  using indigenous species they planted  quick growing exotic species they have  shallow root systems and can't withstand  a typhoon if your aim is to restore the  forest then we should use endemic  indigenous species so that it could help  biodiversity and also the protective  services of the forest is there because  if you use exotics and after 20 years  here comes a big typhoon and they will  all fall down  and all the efforts of restoring is the  school also took a beating Haeju Molina  which is not a native species the heavy  winds of high on knocked it over onto  the roof now the school is missing a  classroom damage like this can be found  across the entire country there's no  telling when money will come in for  repairs  but first the tree has to be removed  reforestation is an important issue here  to make it work the villagers need to  learn more about the forest and what  species are needed the initiative is  supported by the German Agency for  International Cooperation or GI Z they  developed plans for how the land around  the village should be used whenever  possible the hillsides around the  village is to be planted with new trees  and other native plants the men head out  in groups they plant seedlings that they  grew in their own nurseries they've  learned that it's the root system that  counts the roots are what holds the soil  in place it's quite simple if we plant  enough trees here in the area then it  will protect us and our village  now we know that the roots will secure  the soil on the hillside and then we  won't have as many mudslides so planting  trees helps everyone but reforestation  doesn't bring in any extra income the  Vicente family has specialized in  growing ginger they used to sell the  ginger roots at the market but now they  turn the roots into tea the powdered tea  fetches a much better price than just  the roots I sell a lot of the tea I go  from house to house with it  when I come back my daughter asks me how  it went and I tell her it went very well  the Panna initiative also shows people  how to survive and better their lives  without destroying the last remaining  forested areas  the forest is also a habitat for animals  this is a breeding station in  neighboring Milan pi a young Hawk eagle  is being nursed back to health  fishermen found it down by the ocean  they think it may have been swept out by  the typhoon but it's actual habitat is  the forest today it's headed home after  10 more minutes it's time the young bird  is released into freedom everything is  carefully documented the hawk eagle is  an endangered species on the island the  young bird pays no attention it's  getting used to its refound freedom in  one of the few remaining forests of pond  I
 

